he best results  uh and so we mustn't make the mistake of  thinking that the way to compete with  china  is through economic planning and by my  account we now have three plans coming  from the biden administration  uh in the form of major legislation huge  amounts of expenditure  total bill of around six trillion  dollars and i look at that and i ask are  we about to repeat the mistakes  of the 1960s and 70s when it seemed as  if  the only way to compete with the soviet  union was to do economic planning  the way they did that turned out to be a  completely  wrong conclusion and the way the united  states won the cold war was in fact with  with a free market economic model under  ronald reagan uh not with the kind of  thing john kenneth braith was arguing  for in the 1960s  well actually i'm intrigued that you  brought up chinese researchers and  scientists in ai for example  as you know in in america and in the  west in general there's been a greater  suspicion of chinese  nationals or ethnic chinese working  in stem stem areas where do you think  what do you what role do you think they  play in this sort of future competition  between the two great powers or should  they be  welcomed uh into the west or do you  think that we should be  careful being careful about what they  can take back to china  well this is a huge issue in a way that  it wasn't in cold war one there were  relatively few  soviet citizens in the united states at  any point  uh in the cold war that were in the  later part of the cold war exchanges but  they were on a tiny scale compared with  the really huge amount  of exchange that there is between china  and the us  hundreds of thousands of chinese  students until recently at american  universities and often studying  stem subjects a really significant  number of graduate students working in  in key areas such as artificial  intelligence or quantum computing  and it's true to say uh and this is  something that that we know from  uh from the fbi that there has been  espionage as a consequence  we know that the chinese government uses  all kinds of means to try  to hack western technology  breakthroughs often through cyber means  but also through  human intelligence and this creates an  enormous  problem because of course if if one  chinese national working at one big tech  company turns out to be a spy  then suspicion falls uh on the others  and unfortunately that can include  uh american citizens uh often  uh people from chinese of chinese  heritage you've lived in the united  states for  many years so i do think we have to  remember that  one of the bleaker features of american  history in the 20th century was the way  in which  hostility grew towards uh  german americans uh in world war one and  world war ii and of course  in world war ii towards japanese  americans who were treated shamefully  we mustn't repeat that that history and  i i do see dangers  of that a few years ago more than a  decade ago i used the word chimerica to  describe that period of  symbiotic interaction between the us and  china well there are many chimericans uh  and some of them are good friends of  mine these are people who may have been  born in china  but have worked for many years in the  united states and have become american  citizens  when i became an american citizen by far  the largest group  alongside me in an oakland auditorium  where in fact people born in china and  so  we can't cast aspersions on the  commitment  of chinese americans to the country  they've sworn allegiance to if we start  doing that  we're going to be back in the in the  battle days of uh of the 1940s  uh and i of course i'm very conscious  that i'm talking  uh to someone whose surname is is you  and may uh be encountering  uh in the uk some of the kind of uh  anti-asian prejudice that one reads  about  on both sides of the atlantic that can't  be the way this plays out  it is the chinese communist party that  we are concerned  to combat uh in cold water we do not  want the ccp to become more powerful in  the world than it already is  that is quite different from uh  expressing antagonism  to people of of chinese ethnic origin  neil ferguson thank you so much for  joining spectator tv and you can read  neil ferguson's new book  doom the politics of catastrophe which  is out now  if you enjoyed that conversation you  might also enjoy my podcast series  chinese whispers where i look into  chinese politics society and its history  a link should be up on the screen now  [Music]  you
 

